course: arts
lesson: 33
title: Design all around: logos, brands, packaging, fonts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Almost everything you touch was designed by someone: the tick on your trainers, the font in your book, the crisp bag, the road signs and the chair you sit on.
explain: A logo is a small symbol that stands for a company, and a good one is simple enough to recognise on a giant poster and on a tiny phone icon, in colour or in plain black.
explain: Packaging is designed to sell: a shiny bottle can cost double while the shampoo inside is nearly the same, so smart shoppers read the label, not the box.
explain: Some design stories grow bigger than the truth, like "red and yellow make you hungry" or "the apple bite is a computer joke", so it is worth checking what the designer actually said.
explain: People say it their own way: "howay" in the North East means come on, "ya eejit" in Ireland is a cheeky "you idiot", "yeah nah" in Australia means no, and "chatting rubbish" means talking nonsense.
fact: The Nike "swoosh" was designed in 1971 by a design student, Carolyn Davidson, who was reportedly paid 35 dollars; the logo became valuable because of the brand built around it.
fact: Comic Sans was designed in 1994 for Microsoft, inspired by comic lettering; some designers dislike it for serious work, while some readers find it easy to read, so which font fits depends on the job.
fact: Crisp bags are filled with a gas, usually nitrogen, that keeps crisps fresh and stops them crushing; how much air is fair is argued, and the weight on the bag is what you pay for.
fact: Packaging and brand names add a lot to prices, and shop own brands, sold under the shop's own name and usually cheaper, sometimes have very similar contents to big brands.
fact: Club and company logos are protected, so copying one closely for a team or shirt is not allowed, even with one letter changed; designing your own badge is the fair way.
fact: A knock-off copies the look of a famous brand, sometimes with a tiny spelling change, and it is not the real brand.
fact: British road signs were designed in the late 1950s and 1960s by Jock Kinneir and Margaret Calvert, who also designed the lettering, so drivers can read them in a second.
fact: Many fast-food brands use red and yellow because they are easy to spot; the claim that these colours make people hungry is often repeated, but the evidence is weak and argued.
fact: Fine details vanish when a logo is shrunk, so designers test logos at tiny sizes and in one colour.
fact: The designer of the Apple logo has said the bite was there so it looked like an apple and not a cherry; he has said the "byte" joke was not planned.
fact: Good product design is about comfort, strength and use, and a high price can also come from brand and fashion.
exercise: Leo thinks the Nike tick cost millions to design. Which is true? | A student drew it cheaply in 1971 | It cost a million dollars from a top design company in New York | It was painted by a famous artist and sold to Nike at an auction | A museum sold it to Nike
exercise: Why is the Nike logo worth a fortune now? | The brand built around it | Because the original drawing was made with real gold paint | Because it was extremely hard to draw and took years to finish | It has hidden details
exercise: Mia's sister calls Comic Sans a joke. Which is fair? | Fonts suit different jobs | Comic Sans has been banned from all schools in Northern Ireland | There is only one correct font, and every other font is wrong | It is the best font
exercise: Wang Jun's crisp bag is half air. What is the gas for? | Freshness and cushioning | It is only there to cheat buyers into thinking there is more inside | It makes the crisps taste saltier and crunchier as they sit there | It keeps the bag cold
exercise: How can you tell how many crisps you really get? | The weight on the bag | Look at the size of the bag compared with other bags on the shelf | Shake the bag next to your ear and listen to how full it sounds | Count the colours and pictures printed on the front of the packet
exercise: Riya sees two shampoos with nearly the same ingredients. What is wise? | Compare what is inside | Always buy the shiny one, because a fancy bottle must mean better shampoo | Always buy the bottle with the most colours and the biggest picture on it | Never read the label, because the ingredients are only for scientists
exercise: Josh changes one letter of a club badge. What is true? | It is still a copy | Changing just one letter makes the badge his to use however he likes | Club badges belong to nobody, so anyone may copy them freely | Badges can be copied for anything, as long as it is a football team
exercise: Daniel spots an "Adibas" hoodie. What is it? | A knock-off | A brand new branch of the real company, selling cheaper clothes | A real hoodie from the real brand that was printed with a typo | A limited special edition made for collectors in Texas
exercise: Olha notices London road signs all look alike. Why? | One clear designed system | One person travels around painting every sign by hand | Signs are chosen at random from a big catalogue each year | They copy America
exercise: Who designed the British road signs and their lettering? | Jock Kinneir and Margaret Calvert | Carolyn Davidson | Rob Janoff | Vincent Connare
exercise: Brodie says red and yellow are "proven" to make you hungry. Which is true? | That claim is weak and argued | Scientists have proven it beyond any doubt in many experiments | Red and yellow actually make everyone feel a bit sick | They make you sleepy
exercise: Aiden's detailed logo becomes a blob as an app icon. What is the lesson? | Logos must work small | Logos should always be shown huge, never on phones or badges | More detail always makes a better and more memorable logo | Phone apps cannot show any logo properly, so it does not matter
exercise: Mei asks why the Apple logo has a bite. What did the designer say? | To look like an apple | Because someone really took a bite of the apple he was drawing | It is a secret code that only computer experts can understand | It was a print error
exercise: Kofi thinks a chair is too dear. What can a price include? | Design, materials and brand | Only the cost of the wood and nothing else at all | Nothing, prices are picked at random by the shop | Only the shop's rent
exercise: What is a logo? | A symbol that stands for a company | A shop that sells brands | A font for road signs | A gas in crisp bags
exercise: What is an own brand? | A shop's cheaper own-name product | A famous big brand that is made at home by hand | A brand name that you invent yourself for a school project | An illegal brand
test: Your mate goes, "Famous logo, so it must've cost millions to make, innit." What is the best answer? | Many were cheap; value came later | He is right, every famous logo costs millions of pounds to make | Logos are always free, because nobody actually designs them | Only gold logos get famous
test: What would happen if a road sign had tiny, fancy, curly letters? | Drivers could not read it fast | It would be much easier to read from very far away | Drivers would slow down to enjoy reading it, which is safer | It would glow at night
test: A Gen Z kid says the fancy shampoo "ate" ("ate" means did brilliantly) so it must be better. What should she check? | The ingredients | Only the colour of the bottle and how shiny the label is | Whether the bottle feels heavy and expensive in her hand | Whether it has the longest and fanciest name on the shelf
test: In a cartoon, a kid sells "Nikee" shirts and says, "One letter changed, so it's mine." What is wrong? | It still copies the brand | Nothing at all, because changing one letter makes it a new brand | Big brands do not care about copies, so it is completely fine | Shirts are not protected
test: A kid in Perth says, "Yeah nah, red makes you starving, it's science." Which is fair? | The claim is often repeated, but the evidence is weak | It is a proven law of science | Red makes everyone full | Yellow is the hungry colour, not red
test: Your cousin says the Apple bite is "for sure a byte joke, no cap". What is the honest reply? | The designer says it was not planned | He is right, the designer planned the computer joke from the start | Steve Jobs really bit the apple in the photo used for the logo | It was a printing error
test: A crisp bag looks huge but has 30 grams inside. What should you go by? | The weight on the packet | The size of the bag | The picture on the front | The noise when you shake it
test: Mia's teacher says Comic Sans is fine for a party invite but not a serious report. Why? | Fonts carry a mood and suit different jobs | Comic Sans is illegal in reports | Serious reports can only use one font | Party invites must be handwritten
test: Aiden's mate says his band logo is "a brown blob" on a phone. What should Aiden test next time? | How the logo looks tiny and in one colour | How it looks on the biggest wall | How many colours he can add | How long it takes to draw
test: A kid in Dallas says "Adibas" is "just as good, fr" ("fr" means for real). Which is true? | It is not the real brand | It is made in the same factory by the real company | Knock-offs are always better quality than the real thing | Spelling mistakes make it rarer and so much better to own
test: Which is the best way to judge if a chair is well designed? | How comfortable, strong and easy to use it is | How expensive it is | How shiny the label is | How famous the shop is
exam: Identify the principal function of the gas contained within a sealed packet of crisps. | Freshness and protection from crushing | The deception of purchasers as to the true quantity of the contents | The enhancement of the flavour of the contents during storage | Cooling of the contents
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the claim that red and yellow colours induce hunger? | It is frequently repeated, though the supporting evidence is weak and argued | It has been conclusively established by all scientists | It has been formally refuted and is never repeated | It applies solely to the colour blue
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the British road signs of the late 1950s and 1960s. | Kinneir and Calvert designed them | They were painted by hand by a single individual over many decades | They were selected at random from foreign examples without alteration | A computer designed them
exam: Ascertain the consideration most important in testing a logo for small applications. | Its recognisability at a minute size and in a single colour | The quantity of fine detail it contains | Its appearance upon the largest available wall | The number of colours it employs
